          ],
          "content": "124"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.111,
            0.884,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": "Volume measures the amount of three-dimensional space an object or a container occupies. It is measured in cubic units, e.g., cubic meters $( m ^ { 3 } )$ or cubic centimeters (cm<sup>3</sup>). We set the volume of a unit cube (i.e., a cuboid measuring one unit in each direction) to be one cubic unit (e.g., 1 $\\mathrm { m ^ { 3 } }$ or 1 cm<sup>3</sup>, as the case may be). The volume of a general cuboid is the number of unit cubes that fit into it. Accordingly, the formula for the volume $V$ of a cuboid is length × width × height or"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.255,
            0.544,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": "V   =   l w h .",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.292,
            0.544,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": "Compare the formula for the volume of a cuboid with the formula for the area of a rectangle (length × width)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.355,
            0.546,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "Here is a way of understanding why the formula V = lwh is true: think of a cuboid as a large number of thin rectangular pieces (length $l ,$ width w) stacked on top of each other, like a pack of playing cards. (Fig. 14.3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.57,
            0.298,
            0.898,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.691,
                0.468,
                0.778,
                0.488
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 14.3"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.514,
            0.4,
            0.538
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.551,
            0.852,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "Try to work out for yourself why this model explains the formula for the volume of a cuboid, i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.331,
            0.599,
            0.652,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "\\underline { { \\mathrm { v o l u m e } } } = \\underline { { \\mathrm { a r e a } } } \\underline { { \\mathrm { o f } } } \\; \\mathsf { b a s e } \\times \\mathsf { h e i g h t } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.628,
            0.792,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": "Write the formula for the surface area and the volume of a cube."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.672,
            0.676,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "A particular and important case of a cuboid is a cube, whose length, width, and height are the same. This length is the ‘side’ of the cube."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.738,
            0.407,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "Let the side be $a .$ Then:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.763,
            0.577,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "• Total surface area of the cube is $6 a ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.699,
            0.67,
            0.857,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.735,
                0.787,
                0.822,
                0.806
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 14.4"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.788,
            0.456,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": "• Volume of the cube is $a ^ { 3 }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.832,
            0.881,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "Observe how these two formulas are special cases of the formulas 2(wl + hl + hw) and whl, when $w = h = l ,$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
